  <page>

    <exercise>
        <title>Undamped SHM</title>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If a mass m = 0.2 kg is tied to one end of a spring whose force constant k = 80 N/m and 
                        whose other end is held fixed, what are the angular frequency <m>\omega</m>, the 
                        frequency <m>f</m>, and the period <m>\tau</m> of its oscillations? 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If the initial position and velocity are <m>x_0=-1</m> m and <m>v_0=40</m> m/s, what are the
                        constants <m>A,\delta</m> in the expression <m>x(t)=A\cos (\omega t+\delta)</m>? Hint: your 
                        calculator will give you the wrong phase constant: draw a graph of <m>x(t)</m> to figure out the 
                        correct quadrant of <m>\delta</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </exercise>
    
    <exercise>
        <title>Averaging</title>
        <p>
            Consider an undamped simple harmonic oscillator with period <m>\tau</m>. Let 
            <m>\langle f \rangle</m>denote the average value of any variable <m>f(t)</m>, averaged over one complete
            cycle:
            <md>
                \langle f \rangle = \frac{1}{\tau} \int\limits_0^{\tau} f(t) dt
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Show the important results <m>\langle \cos^2(\omega t +\delta)\rangle=0</m> and
                        <m>~{\langle\cos(\omega t+\delta)\sin(\omega t+\delta)\rangle=0}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Critically damped oscillator</title>
        <p>
            Verify that the function <m>x(t)= t e^{-\gamma t/2}</m> is indeed a second solution of the equation of 
            motion for a critically damped oscillator <m>(\gamma/2=ω_0)</m>.
        </p>
        
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>K&amp;K 11.2</title>
        <p>
        </p>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
        <title>General Solution of the Driven Oscillator</title>
        <p>
            Suppose that you have found two particular solutions <m>x_1 (t),x_2(t)</m> of the inhomogeneous equation of 
            motion for a driven damped oscillator: <md>\ddot{x}+\gamma\dot{x}+\omega_0^2x=f(t)</md>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Show that <m>x_1-x_2</m> satisfies the homogeneous equation
                        <md>\ddot{x}+\gamma\dot{x}+\omega_0^2x=0</md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Argue that the general solution to the equation of motion of the driven oscillator is
                        then of the form <m>x(t)=x_p(t)+x_h(t)</m>, where <m>x_p(t)</m> is any particular solution
                        of the inhomogeneous problem that you can find, and <m>x_h(t)</m> is a solution of the
                        homogeneous equation to be determined by the specific initial conditions.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Back to the beginning</title>
        <p>
            We started our discussion of resonance talking about the coffee cup and how to prevent spilling. 
            Solve the differential equation we found in class using the results of this chapter and verify our 
            intuitive results, namely:

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If the spring is very stiff or the frequency of the bumps is very small, the coffee 
                        oscillates with the same amplitude as the road
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If the spring is very soft or the frequency of bumps is very large, 
                        the coffee doesn't oscillate very much
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>

        </p>
        
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Power</title>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <q>Open up</q> our expression for the steady state displacement of the driven oscillator, 
                    <m>X(t)=X_0 \cos⁡(\Omega t+\varphi)</m> into 
                    <md>X(t)=X_1  \cos⁡(\Omega t)+X_2 \sin⁡(\Omega t)</md>, and use the triangle we defined in class 
                    to find <m>X_1,X_2</m> explicitly
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The instantaneous power provided by the driving force is 
                    <m>~{P(t)=F(t)\dot{X}(t)}</m>. Calculate the average power provided by the driving force and check 
                    that it is always positive. Why should that be the case?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Show that the average power has a maximum at resonance <m>\Omega=\omega_0</m>
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Show that <m>X_1=0</m> at resonance
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
        
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>